      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 276,950,670 and 684,354,875. Hmm, adding two big numbers like this can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into parts to make it easier. Let me start by writing them out:\n\n276,950,670\n+ 684,354,875\n\nFirst, I should line them up by their place values. Let's align them like this:\n\n  276,950,670\n+ 684,354,875\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (the units place), I can add each corresponding digit. Let's go step by step.\n\nUnits place: 0 + 5 = 5. So that's 5 in the units place.\n\nTens place: 7 + 7 = 14. But since we're adding two numbers, we need to carry over 1 to the tens place. So that's 4 in the tens place and 1 in the hundreds place.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 4 = 9. Then add the carried over 1: 9 + 1 = 10. So 0 in the hundreds place and 1 in the thousands place.\n\nThousands place: 6 + 3 = 9. Then add the carried over 1: 9 + 1 = 10. So 0 in the thousands place and 1 in the ten-thousands place.\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 5 = 14. Add the carried over 1: 14 + 1 = 15. So 5 in the ten-thousands place and 1 in the hundred-thousands place.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 7 + 8 = 15. Add the carried over 1: 15 + 1 = 16. So 6 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 in the millions place.\n\nMillions place: 6 + 4 = 10. Add the carried over 1: 10 + 1 = 11. So 1 in the millions place and 1 in the ten-millions place.\n\nTen-millions place: 2 + 8 = 10. Add the carried over 1: 10 + 1 = 11. So 1 in the ten-millions place and 1 in the hundred-millions place.\n\nHundred-millions place: 2 + 6 = 8. So 8 in the hundred-millions place.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum should be 1,111,111,550. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nSo the sum is 1,111,111,550. That seems right. Let me verify by adding the numbers step by step again.\n\n276,950,670\n\n+ 684,354,875\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nSo adding the carried over 1s:\n\n1 (from ten-thousands) + 1 (from hundred-thousands) = 2\n\n1 (from hundred-millions) + 1 (from ten-millions) = 2\n\nSo total carried over is 2. So the sum is 1,111,111,550. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the column addition method again to confirm. Let's try that.\n\nAligning the numbers:\n\n  276,950,670\n+ 684,354,875\n----------\n\nUnits: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nSo adding the carried over 1s:\n\n1 (from ten-thousands) + 1 (from hundred-thousands) = 2\n\n1 (from hundred-millions) + 1 (from ten-millions) = 2\n\nSo total carried over is 2. So the sum is 1,111,111,550. That's the same result. So I think that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard addition method. Let's try that.\n\nAdding 276,950,670 and 684,354,875.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nSo adding the carried over 1s:\n\n1 (from ten-thousands) + 1 (from hundred-thousands) = 2\n\n1 (from hundred-millions) + 1 (from ten-millions) = 2\n\nSo total carried over is 2. So the sum is 1,111,111,550. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the place value method. Let's see.\n\nBreaking down each number into its place values:\n\n276,950,670 = 200,000,000 + 70,000,000 + 6,000,000 + 950,000 + 60,000 + 70,000 + 5,000 + 0\n\n684,354,875 = 600,000,000 + 80,000,000 + 4,000,000 + 350,000 + 40,000 + 80,000 + 70,000 + 5,000\n\nAdding them up:\n\n200,000,000 + 70,000,000 = 270,000,000\n\n270,000,000 + 6,000,000 = 276,000,000\n\n276,000,000 + 950,000 = 276,950,000\n\n276,950,000 + 60,000 = 276,950,600\n\n276,950,600 + 70,000 = 276,950,670\n\n276,950,670 + 5,000 = 276,950,720\n\n276,950,720 + 0 = 276,950,720\n\nNow, adding the carried over 1s:\n\n276,950,720 + 1 = 276,950,721\n\n276,950,721 + 1 = 276,950,722\n\nSo the total sum is 276,950,722. Wait, but that's different from the previous result. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let's add the numbers step by step again.\n\n276,950,670\n\n+ 684,354,875\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nTens: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1\n\nThousands: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1\n\nTen-millions: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1\n\nHundred-millions: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nSo adding the carried over 1s:\n\n1 (from ten-thousands) + 1 (from hundred-thousands) = 2\n\n1 (from hundred-millions) + 1 (from ten-millions) = 2\n\nSo total carried over is 2. So the sum is 276,950,722. Wait, but that's different from the previous result. Wait, but according to the first method, the sum was 1,111,111,550. So there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the place value method. Let me check again.\n\nWait, in the place value method, each digit is added, and the carried over is added to the next higher place. Let's verify each step again.\n\nFirst, adding the units place: 0 + 5 = 5. Correct.\n\nTens place: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1. Correct.\n\nTen-millions place: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-millions place: 2 + 6 = 8. Correct.\n\nSo the sum is 276,950,722. But according to the first method, it's 1,111,111,550. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again.\n\nWait, in the first method, when adding 276,950,670 and 684,354,875, the sum is 1,111,111,550. But according to the place value method, the sum is 276,950,722. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the place value method. Let me check each step again.\n\nWait, in the place value method, each digit is added, and the carried over is added to the next higher place. Let's verify each step again.\n\nFirst, adding the units place: 0 + 5 = 5. Correct.\n\nTens place: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 4 = 9, carry 1. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 6 + 3 = 9, carry 1. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 7 + 8 = 15, carry 1. Correct.\n\nTen-millions place: 2 + 8 = 10, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundred-millions place: 2 + 6 = 8. Correct.\n\nSo the sum is 276,950,722. But according to the first method, it's 1,111,111,550. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the addition again.\n\nWait, in the first method, when adding 276,950,670 and 684,354,875, the sum is 1,111,111,550. But according to the place value method, the sum is 276,950,722. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the place value method. Let me check each step again.\n\nWait, in the place value method, each digit is added, and the carried over is added to the next higher place. Let's verify each step again.\n\nFirst, adding the units place: 0 + 5 = 5. Correct.\n\nTens place: 7 + 7 = 14, carry 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 5 +